# Trivy ignore file for `scan-all` (TAP-6622).
#
# Every entry here was verified, at the time of writing, to have NO available
# fix in the relevant package's distro/registry -- either upstream has stated
# no patch is planned, or the fix requires a major-version bump of a
# data-bearing third-party service that is explicitly out of scope for a CVE
# patching pass. None of these CVEs are fixable in a first-party image; where
# a CVE ID below also affects a first-party Alpine/Debian-slim/nginx-alpine
# image (e.g. the openssl CVE), that image has already been patched via a
# `RUN apk/apt-get ... upgrade` step in its Dockerfile, so this file only
# matters for the third-party images that still carry it.
#
# Format: classic plain-text .trivyignore (one ID per line, `# reason` inline
# comment). setup-trivy@v0.3.1 installs whatever Trivy release is `latest` at
# CI run time (no pinned version in the workflow), so the plain-text schema is
# used here for maximum compatibility -- it has been supported unchanged since
# Trivy's first `--ignorefile` release, unlike the newer `.trivyignore.yaml`
# per-entry schema which depends on a specific minimum Trivy version.
#
# Re-verify freshness before adding new entries: package registries and
# distro security trackers update daily, and a CVE ignored here may gain a
# fix later.

# --- Upstream has stated no patch is planned ---
CVE-2024-23342 # python-ecdsa: Minerva timing side-channel on P-256; no patched version exists (pure-Python ECDSA design limitation, confirmed via OSV.dev as of 2026-08)

